<%#
    What a page says when its app cannot start: the entry bundle - the
    frontend's dist/Index.js, or a file it imports - did not load. The
    browser would leave the page blank, and a reload with the server down
    lands on its own error page. Instead the page draws the card a page of
    the app draws when its code cannot download (Common/UI/Components/
    ErrorScreen/PageScreenCard, ChunkLoadRecovery), in the reader's
    language, and loads itself once it can:

      offline          "You're offline", until the browser is back online;
      the server does  "Can't reach the server", asking again every ten
       not answer      seconds while the tab is in view, and on Try now;
      it answers       one reload, which loads the app; a reload less than
                       half a minute ago that did not help says "This page
                       couldn't be loaded", with Reload page - never a loop.

    Plain browser JavaScript, inline: a file of its own would come from the
    server that did not answer. No inline event handlers (a status page's
    Content-Security-Policy refuses them), and nothing it draws comes from
    outside this file. Its comments are EJS comments, so the page carries
    none of them.

    Include it right after <div id="root"></div>, before the entry script:
    the module script whose address ends in /dist/Index.js, which a
    failure to load it or a file it imports names. Locals, all optional:

      languageStorageKey  where the frontend keeps the reader's language
                          (oneuptimeLang for the Dashboard, the Admin
                          Dashboard and Accounts; statusPageLang)
      languageChoiceKey   a key that must say "true" for that language to
                          count: a status page follows its own default
                          language until the visitor picks one
      usesPageLanguage    true: <html lang>, as the server drew it, before
                          the browser's languages (a status page's default)

    The words are the frontends' own (their locale files hold the same
    translations), and the card's styles are ErrorScreenStyle's: Common/
    Tests/Server/Views/EntryLoadFailure.test.ts holds both to them, and runs
    this file as it is.
-%>
<%
  var entrySafeKey = function (value) {
    return typeof value === 'string' && /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/.test(value) ? value : '';
  };

  var entryOptions = {
    storageKey: entrySafeKey(typeof languageStorageKey !== 'undefined' ? languageStorageKey : ''),
    choiceKey: entrySafeKey(typeof languageChoiceKey !== 'undefined' ? languageChoiceKey : ''),
    usesPageLanguage: typeof usesPageLanguage !== 'undefined' && usesPageLanguage === true
  };
-%>
